---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import MediaThumb from '../components/MediaThumb.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Who the Hell Is Don Biggly? | Booze on Hormuz™"
  description="Don Biggly introduces himself, his honorary rank, and the resort his lawyers keep reviewing."
>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="An introduction" title="Who the Hell Is Don Biggly?" />

  <section class="px-6 pb-28 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
    <figure class="card-marble border border-line rounded-xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg max-w-[280px] mx-auto mb-10">
      <MediaThumb src="/don-biggly-portrait.webp" alt="Don Biggly faces the camera in a feathered naval hat and an ornate blue uniform crowded with gold trim and self-awarded medals." aspect="aspect-[4/5]" fit="contain" fallback="Portrait unavailable" sizes="280px" />
      <figcaption class="font-body text-xs text-ink-soft/70 text-center p-2 italic">
        Official portrait. Every medal was awarded by the man wearing it.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="font-body text-lg text-ink-soft space-y-5">
      <p>
        I’m Don Biggly. The most decorated hospitality visionary anywhere near an active
        geopolitical incident. Decorated? We ran out of jacket. Had to put medals on the lining.
        My people checked every metric they were willing to sign. The others were dismissed.
        Very unreliable numbers.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have honorary degrees from very important institutions. They’ve requested privacy.
        Some have requested their seals back. You don’t give somebody a degree and then ask what
        he graduated from. I graduated from success. The ceremony was at my hotel. Everybody paid.
      </p>
      <p>
        They said the hat makes me a captain. I said, &ldquo;Admiral.&rdquo; I awarded myself the
        promotion. Who knows my qualifications better than me? Nobody. The tailor said the
        uniform was the only verifiable part. Beautiful endorsement. We put it on the brochure.
      </p>
      <p>
        Booze on Hormuz started with a coastline. Wonderful coastline. Everybody else saw danger.
        I saw waterfront without competing bids. They said, &ldquo;Don, that’s an exclusion
        zone.&rdquo; I said, &ldquo;Exactly. Finally, somebody understands luxury.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        Now look what we’ve built. A shrimp tower visible from two countries. One country
        complained. We added another level. They’re still looking at it. That’s market penetration.
      </p>
      <p>
        The financing is very sophisticated. You borrow against tomorrow’s headlines. If the news
        gets worse, your view gets better. Our concierge handles luggage, dinner reservations, and
        any question beginning with &ldquo;Can they legally.&rdquo; Same desk. Different jacket.
      </p>
      <p>
        My counsel handles the record. I handle the gold folder. He says I shouldn’t summarize his
        advice. Very jealous of my ability to summarize. I told him we should put
        &ldquo;LEGALLY REVIEWED&rdquo; on everything. He said, &ldquo;Reviewed doesn’t mean approved.&rdquo;
        I said, &ldquo;Then make REVIEWED bigger.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        My philosophy is simple. Be together. Be inspired. Be briefed by counsel. Beautiful card.
        Thick card. You can’t fit it in a wallet, so you have to carry it around. People see my
        name. I thought of that.
      </p>
      <p>
        A fuller biography is coming. I’ve selected a very independent writer. He reports directly
        to me. Every chapter will be checked, improved, and, where necessary, replaced with photographs.
      </p>
      <p>
        We’re doing the cover first. Huge portrait. No room for the charges. Shipping charges.
        My lawyer made me add that.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
